          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Jónsson-Tarski topos
            is ‘the topos analogue of the
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor+space"} Cantor space
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The
            %strong Jónsson-Tarski topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒥
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_2
            is the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%B3nsson-Tarski+algebras"} Jónsson-Tarski algebras
            considered as topos, i.e. its objects are sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            together with an isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to X\times X
            and morphisms are functions that commute with the structure isomorphisms.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒥
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_2
                is an example of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variety+of+algebras"} variety of algebras
                that is also a topos (cf. Johnstone 1985).
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒥
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mi Sh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi J
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_2=Sh(M_2,J)
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
                is the free monoid on two generators
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
                whose only covering family on the unique object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⋅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi a
                        %mo :
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo →
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo :
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo →
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a:\cdot\rightarrow \cdot ,b:\cdot\rightarrow\cdot\}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒥
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_2
                is in a fact a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
                , as a free monoid, is cancellative, hence all-monic and, accordingly,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒥
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_2
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tendue"} étendue
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"}> Peter Freyd
                ). It is discussed from this
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tendue"} perspective
                as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/petit+topos"} petit topos
                for
                %em labeled graphs
                in (Lawvere 1989).
            %li
              %p
                Actually, Freyd observed that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒥
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi Sh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi ℕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_2/F(1)\cong Sh(2^\mathbb{N})
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1)
                the free Jónsson-Tarski algebra on one generator and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\mathbb{N}
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor+space"} Cantor space
                \- this motivates the above quote from Bunge&amp;Funk:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒥
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_2
                looks locally like (the sheaf topos on)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\mathbb{N}
                \!
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi ℕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(2^\mathbb{N})
                classifies ‘ subsets of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                ‘ in the sense that for cocomplete
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
                geometric morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo →
                      %mi Sh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi ℕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}\to Sh(2^\mathbb{N})
                correspond to morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(\mathbb{N})\to\Delta(2)
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℰ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
                the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  constant sheaf functor
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/constant+sheaf+functor"} ?
                (cf.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#MacLaneMoerdijk"} Mac Lane&amp; Moerdijk
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            The idea to consider generalizations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒥
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_2
            seemed to have appeared first in the context of work on étendues (Rosenthal 1981, Lawvere 1989).
          %h3#rosenthals_approach Rosenthal’s approach
          %p
            K. Rosenthal (1981) starts from two basic facts about étendues, namely that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\mathcal{C}^{op}}
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tendue"} étendue
            iff all morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            are monic, and that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(\mathcal{E},J)
            is an étendue if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            is an étendue. His goal is to construct étendues from an all-monic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            by defining a topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\mathcal{C}^{op}}
            from a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H:\mathcal{C}\to Set
            satisfying:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(f)
                is monic ,and
            %li
              %p
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Im
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi Im
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in Im(H(f))\cap Im(H(g))
                then there is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\in\mathcal{C}
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\leq f
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\leq g
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Im
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in Im(H(k))
          %p
            Now given
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in\mathcal{C}
            and a sieve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\in\Omega(X)
            define a sieve
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi cod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo ∧
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi Im
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                  %mi Im
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_X(B):=\{\quad f\in\mathcal{C}| cod(f)=X\quad\wedge\quad Im(H(f))\subseteq\bigcup _{g\in B} Im(H(g))\quad\}.
          %p
            The resulting map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                  %mo :
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j:\Omega\to\Omega
            is a topology.
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi op
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^{op}=\mathcal{M}_2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(X)=2^\mathbb{N}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒥
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_2
          %p
            The generalization to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi op
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^{op}=\mathcal{M}_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Baire+space+of+sequences"} Baire space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔹
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}=\mathbb{N}^\mathbb{N}
            exhibits the
            %strong infinite Jónsson-Tarski topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒥
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_\infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            with an isomorphism to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^\mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(\mathbb{N}^\mathbb{N})
            locally.
          %h3#jnssontarski_toposes_via_profunctors Jónsson-Tarski toposes via profunctors
          %p
            Work on a categorical concept of self-similarity led T. Leinster (2007) to another generalization of the Jónsson-Tarski topos. He starts from the observation that any profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M:\mathcal{A} &amp;#8696;\mathcal{B}
            comes with an adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒜
                        %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℬ
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\otimes M\dashv [M,-]:Set^{\mathcal{A}^{op}}\to Set^{\mathcal{B}^{op}}
            where the left adjoint stems from profunctor composition and the right adjoint is defined for presheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℬ
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in Set^{\mathcal{B}^{op}}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in \mathcal{A}
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi Nat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [ M,X ] (a) := Nat(M(-,a),X)
            i.e. the set of natural transformations from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(-,a)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            or, in other words,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(M(-,a),X)
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℬ
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\mathcal{B}^{op}}
          %p
            For an endoprofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M:\mathcal{A} &amp;#8696;\mathcal{A}
            define a
            %strong Jónsson-Tarski M-algebra
            as a pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\xi)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒜
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in Set^{\mathcal{A}^{op}}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ξ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi
            a natural isomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\overset{\simeq}{\to} [M,X]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒥
                    %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_M
            is a topos since a site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{A}_M,J)
            can be constructed from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            by adjoining new arrows
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi m
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\overset{m}{\to} a
            for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b,a\in\mathcal{A}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\in M(b,a)
            with covers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J(a)
            the set of these arrows (cf. Worrell 2002, Leinster 2007). Furthermore,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒥
                    %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_M
            is monadic over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝒜
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\mathcal{A}^{op}}
          %p
            The classical Jónsson-Tarski topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒥
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_2
            arises from this process by taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M=\mathbf{2}:\mathbf{1} &amp;#8696;\mathbf{1}
            assigning to the unique object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo •
                  %mo ,
                  %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\bullet,\bullet)
            of the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}\times\mathbf{1}
            the two element set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo ,
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\star,\ast\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            is just a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            whence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathbf{2}, X](\bullet)
            is just the set of all maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo ,
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\star,\ast\}\to X
            i. e.
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            and two edges
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo ,
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star,\ast
            i. e. the free monoid on two generators and with coverage
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo ,
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\star,\ast\}
